Z||000000||fierohuddy||05-25-2001||01:37 PM||||OK, I've only been a fiero owner for 2 months and so far, I found out that it's difficult (for me) to change the lock tumbler in the steering column.  I will however persist and succeed.  <P>Now, yesterday, I noticied the take off in first gear seemed to be a little rough.  Not bad yet.  Is this a clutch concern or as someone else mentioned, a "dogbone"... whats a dogbone?.  Then today, I was going up a hill in fourth at about 90kms or 55 mph and the clutch seemed to slip a bit. I eased off on the gas, and it seemed ok.  I'm really hoping this isn't a clutch replacement, but if it is, any suggestions on whether a rookie (me), a very determined to learn rookie, could do this by himself and what are the necessary parts and time requirements.  Heck any info at all will help.  By the way, I don't mind the repairs cause I love Fieros.  Mine is a 1986 V6 Fiero SE.  I have every intention of acquiring a 1988 GT within the next couple of yaers once I have the $$$$.  <P>Thanks so much for all your support and help.  I feel like I'm part of a big old happy family.<P>Simon   ||||reg||9
Z||000001||84Bill||05-25-2001||01:42 PM||||Welcome to the Forum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierohuddy:<BR><B>OK, I've only been a fiero owner for 2 months and so far, I found out that it's difficult (for me) to change the lock tumbler in the steering column.  I will however persist and succeed.  <P>Now, yesterday, I noticied the take off in first gear seemed to be a little rough.  Not bad yet.  Is this a clutch concern or as someone else mentioned, a "dogbone"... whats a dogbone?.  Then today, I was going up a hill in fourth at about 90kms or 55 mph and the clutch seemed to slip a bit. I eased off on the gas, and it seemed ok.  I'm really hoping this isn't a clutch replacement, but if it is, any suggestions on whether a rookie (me), a very determined to learn rookie, could do this by himself and what are the necessary parts and time requirements.  Heck any info at all will help.  By the way, I don't mind the repairs cause I love Fieros.  Mine is a 1986 V6 Fiero SE.  I have every intention of acquiring a 1988 GT within the next couple of yaers once I have the $$$$.  <P>Thanks so much for all your support and help.  I feel like I'm part of a big old happy family.<P>Simon   </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Jeaz man going up a hill and the engine reves up? thats not a good thing even on a good day. I'd hate to be the one to tell you but Yeah sounds like a clutch going south.<P>to change one requires a lot of work and some kind of lifting capability since the engine and trans drop out from the bottom of the car.<BR>take a look<BR> <A HREF="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/pictures.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/pictures.htm</A> <P><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 05-25-2001).]||||reg||11
